    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0060" n="50" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg50"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–1549: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-01-15T14:00:00+08:00">January 15, 1949—2
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 15—1:49 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>120. On evening 13 January, following delivery our <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi>,<anchor n="90" xml:id="d59-f1"/> Generalissimo met with
                        Premier,<anchor n="91" xml:id="d59-f2"/> Vice-Premier,<anchor n="92"
                        xml:id="d59-f3"/> Shao Li-tze,<anchor n="93" xml:id="d59-f4"/>
                    <pb facs="0061" n="51" xml:id="pg51"/> Chang Chun,<anchor n="94" xml:id="d59-f5"
                    /> Chang Chih-chung<anchor n="95" xml:id="d59-f6"/> and Chen Li-fu.<anchor
                        n="96" xml:id="d59-f7"/> In view nature our communication, last three named
                    were directed seek direct approach to Communists. Generalissimo’s departure was
                    also discussed. Indications suggest he may intend leave soon, going Foochow and
                    Amoy and then Taiwan.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d59-f1" n="90" xml:id="d59-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 113, January 14, 6 p. m., <ref target="#pg47">p. 47</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f2" n="91" xml:id="d59-f2-content">
                <p>Sun Fo, president of the Executive Yuan and son of the late Sun Yat-sen.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f3" n="92" xml:id="d59-f3-content">
                <p>Wu Te-chen, concurrently Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f4" n="93" xml:id="d59-f4-content">
                <p>Kuomintang member and Chinese Ambassador in the Soviet Union, 1939–42.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f5" n="94" xml:id="d59-f5-content">
                <p>Military and Political Affairs Director for Southwest China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f6" n="95" xml:id="d59-f6-content">
                <p>Military and Political Affairs Director for Northwest China and Government
                    representative during the 1946 negotiations with the Chinese Communist
                    Party.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f7" n="96" xml:id="d59-f7-content">
                <p>Chinese Minister without Portfolio and coleader of the so-called C-C clique in
                    the Kuomintang.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
